            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, and the majority of the air leakage is most likely coming from the crawlspace. I suggest sealing the belly bag from the crawlspace with an approved air barrier such as Tyvek or Typar. By sealing the belly bag, this should aid with comfort in the home as well. 



Floor vents are another common area for air leakage. When you are tackling the belly bag, if you have access to the furnace ducts, I suggest you seal all of your ducting with metallic tape or mastic. Do not use the grey "Duct Tape". It is not intended for ducting and will dry out and fall off. 



There were some other areas air leakage was observed, through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along doors, as well as around the trim of the wooden framed windows. 



Air leakage was also observed through the rangehood fan. I recommend you replace your rangehood with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 30% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 380&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;16.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 construction with R20 batt insulation. In conjunction with a future upgrade to the exterior siding of this house, I have modelled adding another R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the exterior of all walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding another R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 330&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x8 joists with R28 insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</foundation>
                <windows>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of your current older wood framed windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your 6 wood framed windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 70&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, odors, and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. 



As your home currently has a lot of excess air leakage, natural ventilation has been adequate for fresh air within the home. However with extensive air sealing (such as the belly bag within the crawlspace), this reduces the natural ventilation, and mechanical ventilation becomes necessary. 



Currently, you have an older rangehood fan, which the damper appears to have failed. I recommend you replace your rangehood with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model. These models will incorporate a built-in damper that will close while the fan in not operating.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

If you switch to propane as a fuel source as noted above under the future potential furnace upgrade, I have modelled replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Savings are based on national average hot water use. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Oil - Furnace&lt;/b&gt;

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 92%. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 92% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 190&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Propane - Furnace&lt;/b&gt;

As a future upgrade possibility when your furnace is nearing it's end, I have modelled replacing your current oil fired furnace with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 0&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;16.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Due to recent oil prices, savings are minimal when switching to propane, however historically propane has been a cheaper source of fuel. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Woodstove&lt;/b&gt;



If you decide to install a woodstove, I recommend installing an EPA-certified wood-burning appliance. Ensure that the installation complies with local building, fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an EPA-certified woodstove and using it for 50% of your heat load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; If you harvest your own wood, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,300&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, odors, and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. 



As your home currently has a lot of excess air leakage, natural ventilation has been adequate for fresh air within the home. However with extensive air sealing (such as the belly bag within the crawlspace), this reduces the natural ventilation, and mechanical ventilation becomes necessary. 

Currently, you have an older rangehood fan, which the damper appears to have failed. I recommend you replace your rangehood with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model. These models will incorporate a built-in damper that will close while the fan in not operating.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



